RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.15.0
==========================

This is a major new release of c3p0.

Most deployments will find it a drop-in replacement for prior versions
(the "Incompatible changes", see below, will not affect most users),
but there are a lot of changes and improvements under the cover.

Security-related updates
------------------------

* JndiRefForwardingDataSource, an internal utility of the public JndiRefConnectionPoolDataSource,
  permitted deserialization / dereferencing of JNDI InitialContext environment
  properties which would be used for JNDI lookups without filtering or gating.
  Although prior mitigations dramatically limit how easily an attacker can use
  a malicious InitialContext to do harm, at the very least it can yield
  unexpected outbound network connections from the application and potential
  SSRF attacks. Construction of any InitialContext that even *might*
  derive from deserialization or dereferencing is now gated behind a new
  by-name instantiated policy interface UnsafeInitialContextEnvFilter.
  The default implementation refuses construction of an InitialContext with
  potentially untrustworthy environment. Deployments can provide their own
  more permissive policy via new config parameter

     com.mchange.v2.naming.unsafeInitialContextEnvFilterClassName

* Some of c3p0's security-sensitive parameters can come in via System properties.
  System properties are conventional and convenient for setting JVM-application config,
  but, used naively, they are an inherently insecure mechanism, since any Java code
  anywhere can alter System properties. (Once upon a time, this was supposed to be
  gated by a SecurityManager, but no more. We are still reeling from the traditional
  Java security model being excised from underneath us.) mchange-commons-java now
  introduces SealedSystemProperties, a snapshot of System properties intended to be
  taken very early in the life of an application, and never changeable thereafter.
  c3p0 reads security-sensitive system properties from this sealed snapshot, so
  changes at runtime cannot compromise values set as "-D<prop-name>=value" on start.

* c3p0-0.13.0 introduced a whitelist for which javax.naming.ObjectFactory
  classes c3p0's javax.naming.Reference resolution utilities would construct
  to generate Objects from references. c3p0-0.14.0 introduced a whitelist
  restricting what JavaBean-style classes our own built in JavaBeanObjectFactory
  and C3P0JavaBeanObjectFactory would be willing to construct. Here in c3p0-0.15.0
  we introduce two new whitelists, for by-name-instantiation of classes whose fully
  qualified names we acquired from potentially untrustworthy sources, and a
  whitelist of classes our safer alternative to Java serialization,
  SecurelyStringifiable, is willing to construct. The four current whitelists are

      com.mchange.v2.naming.objectFactory.whitelist
      com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClass.whitelist
      com.mchange.v2.naming.securelyStringifiable.whitelist
      com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.whitelist

  Prior releases used different, now deprecated keys

      com.mchange.v2.naming.objectFactoryWhitelist
      com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClassWhitelist

  If those keys are found, they remain accepted and continue to be used
  in preference to any others, but with a warning to transition to the new names.
  (Thanks to @manus-use on github for suggesting whitelisting by-name instantiation!)

* Fully-qualified classname whitelists -- we now have four of them! --
  have been standardized in implementation and behavior:

  - Each whitelist is specified by a key called "<some-long-prefix>.whitelist".
    That key, and also its subkeys like "<some-long-prefix>.whitelist.something-else",
    expect comma-separated lists of fully-qualified classnames. The union
    of classnames from the top-level whitelist and its subkeys make up
    the whitelist.

  - If the final whitelist contains uniquely the entry "*", whitelist
    enforcement is disabled, and anything goes.

  - If anywhere in the whitelist the token "[]" is found, the whitelist is
    forced empty and the list will always deny whatever it gates.

  - The parent / subkey scheme exists so that different libraries can include
    their own internal classes in the whitelist without application deployers
    having to scan their libraries and collect the library-intended uses.
    However, more neurotic deployments can supersede that more complicated
    and convenient mechanism by using "<some-long-prefix>.overrideWhitelist"
    instead, in which case the whole ".whitelist" and subkey hierarchy is
    ignored, and the whitelist is precisely what is specified (or a unique
    "*" to disable the whitelist, or an "[]" entry anywhere in the list to
    deny all).

  - All whitelist keys and subkeys can be provided both via System properties
    and other configuration. For any given key or subkey, if there are conflicts
    between values derived from System properties and other config, the
    *intersection* of the elements in the conflicting lists becomes the value
    for that key. (If an intersection of lists, at least one of which contained
    more elements, would yield the token "*", however, that result is dropped, it is
    not accepted as an allow-all wildcard.)

* XML is parsed more cautiously should a JVM delegate to a parser that fails to
  support http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl. We now also
  warn if that is the case. (For installations that support,
  http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl, which in practice, should
  be nearly all installations, there is no change in c3p0's default, very cautious
  XML parse. (All caution is thrown to the wind, of course, if a deployment explicitly
  sets "com.mchange.v2.c3p0.cfg.xml.usePermissiveParser=true".)

* Some potential security issues within disused portions of mchange-commons-java
  are addressed (often by deprecating the disused functionality).
  (Thanks @chennbnbnb on github!)

Other updates
-------------

* The build is reorganized in the fashion of a proper mill build.
  c3p0-0.10.0 transitioned the library from ant to mill, but the structure
  of the build was awkwardly hand-ported from ant-style, which used only
  timestamp comparisons to decide whether artifacts needed to be rebuilt.
  mill, more modernly, prefers to cache and hash artifacts, and decides
  what needs to be rebuilt on the basis of those hashes.
  c3p0-0.15.0's build is organized in modern mill style.

* c3p0's statement cache is dramatically improved. Over the years,
  Statements accumulated methods that could potentially affect
  Statement state that c3p0's Statement cache had not been updated
  to revert and reset. The statement cache now properly ensures that
  all of this state is reverted before Statements are made available
  to new clients (or where state cannot be reverted, the statement
  is dropped from the cache). (Thanks Fredrik Jeppson!)

* Thanks to the tools of our brave new world (hi Claude!), we now
  have extensive utilities in the test module for instrumenting and
  exercising the Statement cache.

* The Statement cache is now robust to a "dueling Statement caches"
  condition, where (presumably accidentally), a driver's native Statement
  cache is enabled while c3p0's Statement caching is also enabled, so
  that when c3p0 calls conn.prepareStatement(...) or conn.prepareCall(...)
  it might see instances it has encountered before and is already tracking.
  (Thanks @Devil2-O on github.)

* Miscellaneous rare bugs and leaks are addressed in the Statement cache.

* The codebase is somewhat modernized from its original Java 1.3 style. We
  now utilize, and in the build insist upon, @Override annotations, in both
  c3p0 and mchange-commons-java. The mchange-commons-java has largely been
  upgraded to use generic rather than raw types.

* For config specified via properties-style ClassLoader resources (like
  the standard "/c3p0.properties"), we now merge values from all such
  resources discovered in the classpath, rather than taking only the first
  resource found. The full union of keys is brought into the final config.
  If conflicting values are found for the same key in different variants of
  the resource, the value found on the resource earliest in the CLASSPATH
  is accepted and other values are ignored (with a warning).



New Configuration Parameters
----------------------------

com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.whitelist (and subkeys)
com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.overrideWhitelist
com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.enforceWhitelist

com.mchange.v2.naming.securelyStringifiable.whitelist (and subkeys)
com.mchange.v2.naming.securelyStringifiable.overrideWhitelist

com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClass.whitelist (and subkeys)
com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClass.overrideWhitelist
(Replaces deprecated com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClassWhitelist)

com.mchange.v2.naming.objectFactory.whitelist (and subkeys)
com.mchange.v2.naming.objectFactory.overrideWhitelist
(Replaces deprecated com.mchange.v2.naming.objectFactoryWhitelist)

com.mchange.v2.naming.unsafeInitialContextEnvFilterClassName

Incompatible changes
--------------------

This is a major release, that does include some incompatible changes.
However, the VAST majority of installations are unlikely to directly
use any of the incompatibly modified API.

FOR NEARLY ALL DEPLOYMENTS, c3p0-0.15.0 WILL BE A DROP-IN REPLACEMENT
OF PRIOR VERSIONS.

We work hard to ensure this. (Let us know if we've screwed up.)

You may see warnings in your logs asking you to add any
custom ConnectionCustomizer, ConnectionTester, etc implementations to
com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.whitelist (or any subkey),
and to switch from deprecated whitelist property keys to the new,
more standardized key. Please heed those warnings! In particular,
expect com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.whitelist to
enforce rather than warn in the next major version of c3p0.
Consider setting
com.mchange.v2.reflect.byNameInstantiation.enforceWhitelist=true
now for added safety!

Here are the incompatible changes:

(1) c3p0 uses a construct called SecurelyStringifiable internally as an alternative
    to often unsafe Java serialization. All of these internal uses have been updated
    to a new API, in order to allow imposition of a whitelist so that an attacker
    can't provoke construction of an unexpected class. All of the SecurityStringifiables
    c3p0 uses internally have been to get past the new gate, so these changes should
    be invisible to most installations. Here are the details:

    The duck-typed contract for a "SecurelyStringifiable" used to
    be defined by a class (let's call it `Thing`) implementing
    the following two static methods:

      public static String securelyStringify(Thing thing) throws Exception
      public static Thing constructSecurelyStringified( String stringified ) throws Exception

    The contract has been modified to require

      public static String securelyStringify(Thing thing) throws Exception
      public static Thing constructSecurelyStringified( String stringified, PropertiesConfig pcfg ) throws Exception

    So that we can apply a whitelist defined by configuration to gate which
    classes we construct.

    The securelyStringify(...) method and the serialization format are entirely unchanged.

    Unless you have written your own classes to SecurelyStringifiable's duck-typed
    contract, this change should not affect you.

    (If, a bit shockingly, you *have* written custom SecurelyStringifiables, you'll
    need to update the signature of constructSecurelyStringified and add your class to
    "com.mchange.v2.naming.securelyStringifiable.whitelist" or a subkey. You are
    unlikely to need to modify your implementation. SecurelyStringifiable applies
    the whitelist gating prior to the per-class methods; the updated method signature
    is just to make configuration available on reconstruction should implementers
    desire it, since it must be provided anyway for the whitelist gating.)

(2) Construction of InitialContext instances against environments that may not be
    trustworthy -- because they might have resulted from deserializing objects or
    dereferencing javax.naming.Reference instances that may be subject to
    tampering -- is always gated now by an instance of UnsafeInitialContextEnvFilter.
    In particular, lookups against a non-default InitialContext environment to
    JndiRefConnectionPoolDataSource will always be gated. The default implementation
    of the gate refuses everything. You can supply your own more permissive
    policy by implementing the single-method UnsafeInitialContextEnvFilter interface
    and deploying it via com.mchange.v2.naming.unsafeInitialContextEnvFilterClassName
    in your config.

(3) Although this was kind-of sort-of implicitly expected previously, we now formally
    insist that by-name-instantiated policy classes, including ConnectionTesters,
    ConnectionCustomizers, NameGuards, UnsafeInitialContextEnvFilters, etc that are
    instantiated via their public, no-arg constructor must be stateless, such that any
    instance is interchangeable with any other. (We tend to cache these instances rather
    than recreating them all the time.) Previously we reset the ConnectionTester cache
    on (very rare) runtime refreshes of c3p0's configuration
    [via C3P0Config.refreshMainConfig(...)]. We no longer do so, since ConnectionTester
    implementations are now required to be stateless (and c3p0's internal
    com.mchange.v2.c3p0.impl.DefaultConnectionTester now conforms)

(4) In the unusual event that an installation relied upon changing configuration via
    System properties at runtime, after c3p0's configuration had been initialized,
    c3p0's use of SealedSystemProperties for security-sensitive configuration may be a
    breaking change.

(5) Since, until this release, only the first "/c3p0.properties" resource found in a
    CLASSPATH was used, it has been rare for libraries to ship their own c3p0 config,
    so in practice this is very unlikely to bite. But, should an installation have
    "/c3p0.properties" or other classloader-resource properties configuration
    sources that c3p0 reads (which include also "/mchange-commons.properties",
    "/mchange-log.properties", and "/c3p0-default.properties"), you might experience
    unexpected introduction of new configuration keys, since multiple instances of
    the same resource path in config are now merged, rather than all but the first
    found being ignored.